---
import DocLayout from '../layouts/DocLayout.astro';
import { AUTHOR } from '../config/author';
import { SITE } from '../config/site';
import { authorPath } from '../lib/seo';
---

<DocLayout
  title={`About ${SITE.name}`}
  description={`${SITE.name} is a free, private CTC to in-hand salary calculator for India, with rates checked against official sources for ${SITE.shortYearLabel}.`}
>
  <p>
    {SITE.name} answers one question well:{' '}
    <strong>
      of the CTC on your offer letter, how much actually reaches your bank account each month?
    </strong>
  </p>

  <h2>Why another salary calculator</h2>
  <p>
    Most CTC calculators get the easy part right and quietly skip the parts that change the answer:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Both tax regimes, side by side.</strong> You see the new and old regime results
      together, with the difference, rather than one assumed regime.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The Labour Code wage rule, read correctly.</strong> Since November 2025, if your
      allowances come to more than half your pay, the excess counts as wages for PF, gratuity and
      ESI. Contrary to what is widely written, there is no rule forcing Basic to be half of CTC. We
      apply the actual rule and tell you when it changed your numbers.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Marginal relief done properly.</strong> Tax just above Rs 12 lakh, Rs 50 lakh and
      higher thresholds is capped the way the law requires: crossing a threshold costs at most the
      income above it, plus the 4% cess. Many calculators skip the cap entirely.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Professional tax by state, with the February rule.</strong> Maharashtra and Karnataka
      charge Rs 300 in February. Where we have not verified a state's slabs, we say so instead of
      guessing.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How we keep it accurate</h2>
  <p>
    Every statutory rate is checked against the source that sets it: the Income-tax Act, 2025, the
    Finance Act 2026, the Income-tax Rules 2026, Ministry of Labour and ESIC notifications, and the
    state professional tax Acts. Where we could not reach an official source, we say so rather than
    presenting a figure as settled. The footer shows when the rates were last checked, and the
    calculation code is covered by tests built from hand-worked examples. The full method, including
    what we could not verify, is on the <a href="/how-it-works">How it works</a> page.
  </p>

  {AUTHOR && (
    <>
      <h2>Who builds it</h2>
      <p>
        {SITE.name} is built and maintained by <a href={authorPath(AUTHOR)}>{AUTHOR.name}</a> with
        the help of AI coding tools. Every figure is traced to the law or notification that sets it.
        It is an independent project with no sponsors.
      </p>
    </>
  )}

  <h2>Private by design</h2>
  <p>
    Calculations run in your browser. Your salary is never sent to a server. See the{' '}
    <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Found a mistake?</h2>
  <p>
    Please <a href="/contact">tell us</a>. Corrections are fixed first.
  </p>
</DocLayout>
